Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, saying, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him. And he put the child in the midst of them.
Jesus said, "Truly I tell you, unless you turn and become like children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever accepts a little child like this in my name accepts me.
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me were to be led into sin? If he does that, it would be better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
How terrible it will be for the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Things like this must come. But how terrible it will be for those who cause them!
If your hand or foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter life maimed than to have two hands or two feet and be thrown into eternal fire.
If your eye causes you to stumble, pluck it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the fire of hell. (Matthew 18:9) The parable of the lost sheep.
Look, do not despise one of these little ones. Behold, I say to you: Their angels in heaven always behold the face of my Father who is in heaven.
I
What do you think? If a man has a hundred sheep and one of them goes astray, will he not leave the ninety-nine on the mountains and go and search for the one that went astray?
What I am about to tell you is true. If he finds that sheep, he will be happier with it than with ninety-nine other sheep that did not get lost.
Thus, your father who is in heaven. He does not want one of these little ones to perish. When someone sins against you.
If your brother wrongs you, go and rebuke him between you and him. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother.
But what if he does not listen to you? Then take one or two more with you, that every word may be established on the mouth of two or three witnesses.' (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then report it to the church. And what if he even refuses to listen to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat an irreligious person or a tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is a reality. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Again, here is what I tell you: "Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them."
"Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them." The servant who did not have compassion.
Peter came to Jesus and asked him, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
The kingdom of the heavens is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
While the king was about to do that, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man could not pay. So his master issued an order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed were to be sold to pay his debts.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
The lady felt sorry for him, so she forgave him for what he had been and set him free.
But then that waiter came out and found one of the other waiters to whom he owed a few dollars. He grabbed him and began to strangle him. "Pay me what you owe me!" he said.
The other server fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded with him. "I will pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and imprisoned the man until he paid him what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened. This disturbed them greatly. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the master called the first servant and said to him, "You wicked servant! Did I not show mercy to you and cancel all that debt of yours because you pleaded with me?"
Haven't you been the one who should show mercy to the stumbling servant, just as I have shown mercy to you?
In anger, his master handed him over to the jailers. He will be punished until he pays back all his debts.
Thus does my Father who is in heaven treat you if you do not forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus finished these words, he left Galilee and went into the regions of Judea beyond the Jordan.
Large crowds followed him, and he healed them there.
The Pharisees came to entrap him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for every cause?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that the Creator from the beginning made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave to his wife; and they shall become one flesh' (Genesis 2:24).
They are no longer two but one flesh. Let no one separate what God has joined together.
They asked, "Then why did Moses command that a man give his wife a certificate of divorce and send her away?"
Jesus answered: "Because of the hardness of your hearts Moses allowed you to divorce your wives, but it was not like this from the beginning.
This is what I am telling you: Whoever divorces his wife and marries another commits adultery. A man is not permitted to divorce his wife unless she has been unfaithful to him.
So the disciples said to him, "If such is the case of a man with his wife, it is better not to marry!"
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who have been helped to live without marriage can accept that.
Some men are unable to have children because they were born that way. Others became that way at the hands of other people. And there are those who made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living in this way, let them do so." The little children were brought to Jesus.
Many children came to Jesus, and the disciples did not hinder them. But Jesus called the children to Him and said, "Let the little children come to Me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these."
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them. For the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus placed his hands on them, then he left. Jesus and the Rich Young Man.
A man approached Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what is the great commandment I must observe to inherit eternal life?"
Jesus answered, "Why do you ask me about what is good? There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, then keep the commandments."
Jesus replied, " 'You shall not murder; You shall not commit adultery; You shall not steal; You shall not bear false witness; Honor your father and mother ' and 'You shall love your neighbor as yourself ' "
Honor your father and your mother. (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) And "You shall love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commandments, so what else must I do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. Then you will have treasure in heaven. And come, follow me."
When the young man heard that, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Jesus said to his disciples: "Truly I tell you: it is difficult for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.
Again I say to you: It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were very astonished and said, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "With people, this is impossible; but with God, all things are possible."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What will be our reward?"
Jesus said to them, "Truly, I say to you, you who have followed me, in the regeneration, when the Son of Man sits on the throne of his glory, you also will sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.
Everyone who has left houses, families, or fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more. They will also inherit eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who was a landowner. He went out early in the morning to go to his field to hire workers to work in his vineyard.
And it happened that he would give them the usual wage for a day's work for the workers. Then he sent them to his vineyard.
At about nine in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market doing nothing.
So he said to them, "You also go to my vineyard, and I will give you what is fair."
So they left. "He went out again around twelve and three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock he went out and found others standing around him. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long without working?"
He answered, "Because no one has hired us." And he said to them, "You too go into the vineyard and whatever is right you will receive."
In the evening, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the manager over the workers and said, "Go call the workers and give them their wages, starting with the last ones who you hired first, then move to the first ones."
The workers who were hired came around five o'clock. Each of them received the usual day's wages.
So when those hired first came, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received the usual day's wage.
When they took possession, they started complaining about the owner.
They said, "These recently hired people only worked for one hour, yet you paid them the same as you paid us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day."
The landowner answered one of them: "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and go. I want to give the ones I recently hired the same wage I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my money? Do you feel deceived because I gave generously to others?
Therefore, those who are last will be first, and those who are first will be last." Jesus speaks again of his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve aside to talk to them.
He said, "We are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews; they will mock him and flog him and crucify him. And on the third day he will rise from the dead!" A mother asks Jesus for a favor.
The mother of my sons Zebedee came to him, and she had her two sons with her. She bowed down before him and made a request of him.
Jesus said, "What do you want?" She said, "That you sit with these, my two sons, one at your right hand and one at your left, in your kingdom."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" They said, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "You will indeed drink from my cup, and yet you will not sit down to rule with me. For the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many."
The other ten students heard about this. They became angry at the two brothers.
Jesus called them to Him and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them.
Do not be like that. Instead, everyone among you who wants to be great must be your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many." Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and His disciples were leaving Jericho, and a great crowd was following Him.
Two blind men sitting by the roadside, when they heard that Jesus was passing by, called out, saying, "Have mercy on us, O Lord, O Son of David!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the men cried out even louder, saying, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them. "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked them.
They replied, "Our lord, we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep compassion for them. He touched their eyes. And immediately they saw and followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
And when they drew near to Jerusalem, and came to Bethphage at the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples.
And he said to them, "Go to the village before you. And when you enter it, you will find a tethered donkey, on which is a saddle. Untie the donkey and bring it to me."
If anyone says anything to you, say, 'The Lord needs them. The owner of the house will send them out immediately.'"
And all this took place to fulfill what was spoken by the prophets.
Say to the city of Zion's daughter, "Behold, your king is coming to you; righteous and victorious is he, and he is riding on a donkey, even on a colt, the foal of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
So the disciples went and did as Jesus had commanded them.
They brought a donkey and a young donkey, placed their clothes on them, and Jesus sat on them.
A large crowd gathered and they placed their garments on the road. Others cut branches from the trees and placed them on the road.
Some of the people went ahead of him, and some of them followed him. And they were crying out, saying: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. And the people said, "Who is this?"
The multitude answered, "This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth of Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple of God. And He cast out all those who were selling and buying in the temple, and He overturned the tables of the moneychangers and the chairs of those who sold the doves.
«It is written: 'My house shall be a house of prayer,' for all the nations.'» (Isaiah 56: 7) ««And you have made it a den of thieves.»» (Jeremiah 7: 11).
He came to the temple, and there were blind and lame people, and they were speaking.
The chief priests and the scribes saw the wonderful things he did, and the children were shouting in the temple, saying, "We want no more of this! We want no more of this! Give us Barabbas!" The priests and the scribes heard the noise and said, "What is this? This is a bad thing!" And the captain of the temple guard and some of the high priests went to the crowd and said, "Why are you doing this? If you want to bring him out, tell us, and we will not stop you." Then the high priests and the scribes stood up and said, "We have not found him guilty of any of these things he has done. But we have a law, and by that law he must die, because he has claimed to be the Son of God." When the high priests and the scribes heard this, they shouted, "We have found him guilty of death! What more do you need? Let us bring him out to the people, so they can all see him and know that he is not the Messiah they were expecting." And the crowd began to shout, "Yes, let him come out to us, so we can see him for ourselves and know that he is not the Messiah!" And the high priests and the scribes looked at each other and said, "This is not going well. We need to do something to stop this from getting out of hand." And one of the high priests said, "I have an idea. Let us go to the governor and tell him that this man is causing trouble and is claiming to be a king. Perhaps the governor will agree to have him executed and that will be the end of it." And the high priests and the scribes thought that this was a good plan, so they went to the governor and told him everything they had planned.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read in the Scriptures? It says, 'I have made even the little children praise you.'" (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the crowd and went out of the city to Bethany, and there he spent the night. The fig tree dried up.
Early in the morning, Jesus was going to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He came to it but found nothing on it but leaves. He said to it, "May you never again come into fruition!" And immediately the fig tree withered.
And when the disciples saw it, they marveled, saying, "How did the fig tree wither immediately?"
Jesus answered and said to them, "Truly I say to you, that whoever says to this mountain, 'Be removed and cast into the sea,' and does not doubt in his heart, but believes that what he says will be, whatever he says will be for him. Therefore I say to you, whatever things you ask when you pray, believe that you receive them, and they will be yours."
If you believe, you will receive what you are asking for in prayer." The Sultan of Jesus is being impugned.
Jesus entered the temple courts driving out all who were selling and buying in the temple courts, and he overturned the tables of the money changers and the benches of those who sold doves, and he said to them, "It is written, 'My house shall be called a house of prayer,' but you have made it a den of robbers."
Jesus answered, "I will ask you one question. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things.
From where did John's baptism come? Did it come from heaven? Or from men?" They discussed among themselves about that. They said, "If we say: From heaven, he will ask us: So why did you not believe in him?"
But what if we say, "Of the people"? We fear people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet."
Jesus answered: "I do not know." Jesus said: "And I also will not tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
The son answered, "I will not," but then he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied, "I will do it, sir." But he did not go.
Translate the following text into English, only print out the translation, not add any explanations: Translate the following text into English: "Which of the two sons did what his father wanted?" They answered: "The first." Jesus said to them: "Truly I tell you: tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the kingdom of God ahead of you." The translation is: **"Which of the two sons did what his father wanted?" They answered: "The first." Jesus said to them: "Truly I tell you: tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the kingdom of God ahead of you."**
John came to show you the true way to live. And you did not believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. You saw this. But even then you did not turn away from your sins and believe him. The parable of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned a piece of land, so he planted a vineyard in it. He built a wall around it and dug a wine press in it. He also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to some farmers, then he traveled far away.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. They punched one of them. They killed another. And they threw stones at the third to kill him.
Then the man sent other slaves to the tenants, and he sent more than he had sent at the first. And the tenants treated them in the same way.
Finally, he sent his son to them. "They will respect my son," he said.
But the tenants saw the son coming. And they said to each other, "This is the one who will inherit all the owner's possessions someday. Let's kill him, and then everything will be ours."
And they took him and brought him out of the vineyard and killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do with these tenants?
"They will destroy those evil ones," they answered. "Then he will rent the vineyard to other tenants. They will give him his share of the produce at harvest time."
Jesus said to them: "Have you not read in the Scriptures: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This was in the Lord's mind, and it is wonderful in our eyes'?" (Psalm 118: 22-23).
Here is what I say to you: The kingdom of God will be taken from you, and given to a people who will bear fruit.
Anyone who falls on that stone will be crushed into pieces. But the stone will crush anyone who falls on it.
The chief priests and the Sadducees heard Jesus' parables. They knew that he was speaking about them.
So they searched for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd, because the people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. "He said,
Thus is the kingdom of heaven like. A king who prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to those invited to the dinner. And the servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent other slaves, saying, 'Tell those who are invited: I have prepared my dinner, and I have killed my fattened calves. Everything is ready. Come to the feast!'
But the people did not pay attention. One of them went to his field, and another went to his work.
The rest of them seized his servants, mistreated them, and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
The king said to his servants: "The feast is ready, and the guests were not deserving of attendance."
Go to street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you can find.
The servants then went out to the roads and gathered all those they found, whether they were good or evil. And the wedding hall was soon filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
He said, "My friend, how did you get in here without your wedding clothes?" The man could not think of anything to say.
The king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet, and throw him into the outer darkness, where there is weeping and gnashing of teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen." Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians how they might seize Jesus by his words.
They sent their disciples to him, along with the Herodians. And they said to him, "Teacher, we know that you are true, and that you teach the way of God in truth, and that you do not care for anyone, nor do you commend anyone."
So tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar, or not?
But Jesus knew their evil plans. He said to them, "Hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes. Bring him a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
"For Caesar," they answered. And He said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were amazed and left him and went away. Marriage has not yet raised up the dead.
On that same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name.
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened with the second and third brothers. It happened until it reached the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, whose wife will she be? For all seven have married her.
Jesus answered, "You are wrong, because you do not know the Scriptures or the power of God.
When the dead rise, they will not marry, nor will their fathers give them away in marriage; for they will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the walking dead? Have you not read what God has told you?
"I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) "He is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living."
When the crowds heard this, they were astonished at his teaching. The greatest commandment is
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees had not been able to answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them, an expert in the law, tested Jesus with a question.
And he said to him, "Teacher, which commandment is the greatest in the law?" (Mk 12:28)
Jesus answered, "You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind" (Deuteronomy 6: 5).
This is the first and most important commandment.
And a second resembles it: "Love your neighbor as yourself" (Leviticus 19:18).
Everything written in the law and the prophets must be fulfilled by these two commandments." So who is the son of God?
The Pharisees met together, and Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Christ? Whose son is he?" They answered, "The son of David."
Why then does David call him Lord?
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him "my Lord," how then could he be his son?
No one could answer him with a single word. From that day on, no one dared to ask him any questions again.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The Pharisees and Sadducees sat on the seat of Moses."
So you must obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But don't do what they do. They don't practice what they preach.
They tie heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of others. But they themselves are not willing to move a single finger to move them.
All that they do, they do to be seen by men. They carry on their foreheads and arms small boxes containing verses from the Holy Book. They make these boxes very broad. And they make the fringes of their garments very long.
They love to sit at the heads of tables at banquets, and to take the first chairs in assemblies.
They like to be greeted in the markets. And they like people to call them "Ya Mu'allem" (O teacher/master).
But none of you should be called 'Rabbi,' because you have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth your father, for one is your Father, the one in heaven.
You should not be called teachers, for One is your Teacher, and you are all brothers.
The greatest among you is the servant of you.
Whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and whoever is humbled will be exalted.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you shut the kingdom of heaven in men's faces, for you neither enter into the kingdom of heaven, nor allow those who are entering to go in.
I
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you travel across sea and land to win over one proselyte, and when you have succeeded you make him twice as much a child of hell as yourselves.
Woe to you, blind guides, who say: 'If anyone swears by the temple, it is nothing; but if anyone swears by the gold of the temple, he must fulfill his vow.'
You are blind and stupid! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or the temple that sanctifies the gold?
You also say, "If someone swears by the altar, there is nothing wrong; but if he swears by the food on it, he is bound."
O ye blind! Which of these two is more important? Is it the gift? Or is it the altar which sanctifies the gift?
Whoever, therefore, swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And whoever swears in the temple, let him swear in his temple with the one who is sitting in it.
And whoever swears by the heaven has indeed sworn by the throne of Allah and by whoever is on it that verily it is the truth and whatever is between them.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you tithe mint, dill, and cumin, and neglect the weightier matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faith. These ought to have been done without neglecting the others.
O blind guides! You strain out a gnat and swallow a camel!
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you clean the outside of the cup and the plate, but inside they are full of greed and self-indulgence. You are like tombs, whitewashed on the outside but inside full of dead men's bones and everything unclean. Even so, on the outside you appear righteous to people, but God knows your hearts. What sorrow awaits you scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you build tombs for the prophets and decorate the tombs of the righteous. And you say, 'If we had lived in the days of our ancestors, we would not have participated with them in shedding the blood of the prophets.' So you testify against yourselves that you are the descendants of those who murdered the prophets. Fill up then the measure of your fathers' sins! Serpents! Generation of vipers! How can you escape being sentenced to hell? For I tell you, unless your righteousness surpasses that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. You are in for trouble in the end! For it is as easy for a camel to go through the eye of a needle as it is for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. It is easier for heaven and earth to disappear than for the smallest mark of a letter in the law to be changed. Whoever relaxes even the smallest of these commandments and teaches others to do the same will be called the least in the kingdom of heaven. But whoever obeys even the smallest of these commandments and teaches others to do the same will be called the greatest in the kingdom of heaven. For I tell you that unless your righteousness surpasses that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. You are in for trouble in the end! For it is as easy for a camel to go through the eye of a needle as it is for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. It is easier for heaven and earth to disappear than for the smallest mark of a letter in the law to be changed. Whoever relaxes even the smallest of these commandments and tea