Oh, blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and dish, so that the outside of them may also be clean.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs, which outwardly appear beautiful, but within are full of the bones of the dead and of all impurity.
The same applies to you. From the outside, it seems that you are doing what is right. But on the inside, you are filled with what is wrong. You are pretending to be what you are not.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the tombs of the righteous,
And you say, "If we had lived in the days of those who preceded us, we would not have done what they did. We would not have helped in the killing of the prophets."
So you yourselves are the witnesses that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
Complete, then, the sins that those who lived before you began!
O den full of poisonous snakes! How will you escape being sentenced to the fire?
Therefore, I send you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, and you will crucify others. And others you will flog in your synagogues. And you will persecute them from city to city.
So you are making atonement for the blood of all the righteous person shed on the earth, from the blood of Abel the righteous person to the blood of Zechariah the son of Barachiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.
What I am about to tell you is a reality. All this will happen to those who are now living.
O Jerusalem! O Jerusalem! Slayer of prophets and stoner of those sent to her! How many times did I want to gather your children as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, and you did not call me!
Look, your house was left empty.
I tell you: You will not see me until the time comes when you say, 'Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord' (Psalm 118:26).
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. As he was walking away, his disciples came to him, wanting to call his attention to the temple buildings.
Jesus said, "Do you see all these things? Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left here upon another, but all will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. And there came to Him the disciples privately, saying, "Tell us, when shall these things be? And what shall be the sign of Your coming, and of the end of the age?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Be vigilant, lest someone deceive you.
Many will come in my name, and they will say, 'I am the Messiah!', and they will lead many astray.
You will hear about wars. You will also hear people talking about future wars. Do not be alarmed. Those things must happen. But the end is not yet.
One nation wars against another nation, and one monarchy wars against another monarchy. And people go hungry. And earthquakes happen in many places.
All of these are the beginning of labor pains.
Then they will hand you over to be afflicted and killed. All nations will hate you for my name's sake.
At that time, many will fall away from their faith. Some will hate each other. And they will hand each other over to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear, and they will lead many people astray.
Because evil will grow, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever endures to the end, that one will be saved.
This gospel of the kingdom shall be preached in all the world for a witness unto all nations. Then shall the end come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that is destroyed" (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11). One day you will see it standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Let those who are in Judaism flee to the mountains.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to get anything.
Nobody in the field should have to come back to get their coat.
How intense a day is for a pregnant woman and a nursing mother!
I pray that you do not have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible torments in those days, worse than have ever been since the beginning of the world until now, and there will never be anything like it again.
If time had not been shortened, no one would have lived. But because of the chosen people of God, it will be shortened.
At that time, someone might say to you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or "Here he is!" Do not believe it.
False messiahs and false prophets will appear. They will perform signs and great wonders. They will try to deceive the elect people of God, if possible.
Look, I already told you.
So when anyone says to you, 'He is in the wilderness,' do not go out; or if they say, 'He is in the inner chambers,' do not believe it.
The lightning that comes from the east will be seen in the west. And so it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Predators gather wherever a carcass is found.
Immediately after the terrible sufferings of those days, 'the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give its light. The stars shall fall from heaven, and the powers of the heavens shall be shaken.' (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
At that time, the sign of the Son of Man will appear in the sky. All the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of the sky, with great power and glory.
He will send His angels with a great trumpet sound. They will gather His chosen people from all four directions. They will bring them from the end of the heavens to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. Once its branches become tender and its leaves are sprouting, you know that summer is near.
Similarly, when you see all those things happening, you know the end is near. It's at the doorstep.
What I am about to tell you is true. The people living at that time will certainly not die until all those things have been fulfilled.
His heaven and his earth shall perish; but my words shall never be abolished. The day of the hour and its hour - none knows them except God.
No one knows that day or hour. Even the angels in heaven do not know. The Son does not know; only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. So also will it be in the day of the Son of Man.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day that Noah entered the ark.
They had no idea what was going to happen until the flood came and swept them all away. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in a field, one will be taken and the other left.
Six grains are taken with a manual plough, one is taken and the other is left behind.
So be on your guard. They do not know on what day their Lord will come.
You must understand one thing. If the homeowner knew at what hour of the night the thief would come, he would have stayed awake. He would not have allowed his house to be broken into.
Therefore, just as you are ready, be ready also, because just as the bridegroom is ready to return from the feast, so will the Messiah be revealed from heaven with His powerful angels in blazing fire, dealing out vengeance to those who know God's law but deliberately choose to break it, giving them the reward they deserve. Beloved ones, do not be surprised at the painful trial you are suffering, as though something strange were happening to you; but remember that you are being personally tested by the Messiah, our loving God and Savior, who is deeply concerned about you and is watching closely to see how you will respond to this difficult challenge. Therefore, do not look at your problems from your own limited human perspective, but look to God with faith and patience, knowing that He is in complete control and will ultimately work everything out for the good of those who love and trust Him. So encourage one another with these words, reminding each other of God's unchanging promises and His deep love for us, which He demonstrated by sending His only Son into the world to die for our sins and rise again, offering us forgiveness, eternal life, and a personal relationship with God our Heavenly Father. Therefore, let us draw near to God with a sincere heart and full assurance that we will find Him, because He is not far from any of us. And let us hold firmly to the hope we profess, for by doing this we bring glory to God. So let us consider carefully how we may spur one another on toward love and good works, not neglecting to meet together, as some people do, but encouraging one another, especially as you see the Day approaching. Therefore, be alert and strengthen your hearts, for as you listen, you will understand what is said. So listen carefully to the words of this covenant, and take them to heart, because you are showing that you are aware of the great and awesome God, our Lord, who is speaking to you face to face at this mountain. And because you have kept all my commandments and have not omitted them among all your people, therefore you are to be shown all these words, in order to charge your children to be careful to do all the words of this Law. For it is not a vain thing for you, because it is your life, and through this law you will long endure. So be careful to do everything that is written in it, because by doing so you will be showing that you are wise and discerning in the eyes of all the nations. And when these nations ask you, 'Why did the Lord your God do this to these nations that you have dispossessed?' you will say to them, 'It is because the people of these nations did not worship the Lord their God, or observe the statutes and rules that he gave them. Therefore the Lord made them a curse, just as he had warned them. And you, be careful not to be cursed like this, but obey the commandments of the Lord your God, which I am commanding you today for your own good.' " 'Now it came about, when Moses had finished writing the words of this law in a book, until they were completed, that Moses commanded the Levites, who were in charge of the tent of meeting, saying, "Take this book of the law and place it beside the ark of the testimony, in the inner part of the ark of the testimony. And there it is to be as a witness against you and against your children. For according to all the words of this law, which they have before them continually, it will be, that if they do all that I command them to do, it will be well for them; it will be well for them forever and ever. But if they will not obey the voice of the Lord their God, to observe all his commandments which I have commanded them, and to the statutes and rules that the Lord their God has commanded them, then the Lord will make
Let us suppose that a certain master puts one of his servants over the other servants in his house. The business of this servant is to give them their food at the proper time. The master wants a servant that is faithful and wise for this matter.
It will be good for the servant if his master finds him doing his work when the master returns.
What I am telling you is true. The master will make that servant ruler over all that he has.
But what if that server is evil? What if he says to himself: "My master is very late in returning."
Let's suppose he begins by striking the other servants. And let's suppose he eats and drinks with those who drink to excess.
The master of that servant will return on a day unexpected by the servant. He will return at an hour unknown to the servant.
Then his master will cut him to pieces. He will send him to the place where the accused are sent. There the people will groan and grit their teeth.
The Parable of the Ten Virgins.
This is what the Kingdom of Heaven will be like at that time. Ten virgins took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were fools. Five of them were wise.
The silly women took their lamps, but they did not take olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in bottles with their lamps.
The groom did not come for a long time. So, all the bridesmaids got tired and slept.
At midnight someone shouted, "This is the groom! Come out to meet him!"
Then all the bridesmaids woke up and prepared their lamps.
The foolish women said to the wise women, "Give us some of your oil, because our lamps are going out."
"No," they replied. "There may not be enough for everyone. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy for yourselves."
They went out to buy oil. While they were going, the bridegroom arrived, and the invited guests entered the wedding, and the door was closed.
Later, the other bridesmaids came too. "Sir! Sir!" they said, "Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Therefore, be ready, because you do not know at what hour the master will come. The Parable of the Three Servants
Again, the Kingdom of Heaven is like this: A man going on a journey called his servants and entrusted his property to them.
He gave ten thousand dollars to one. And he gave four thousand dollars to another. And he gave two thousand dollars to the third. The man gave each servant the amount he knew the servant could take care of. Then he went on his journey.
The server who received the ten thousand dollars immediately went and invested his money. He earned an additional ten thousand dollars.
Whoever earned 4000 dollars earned an additional 4000 dollars.
But the man who took two thousand dollars went and dug a hole in the ground and hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of these servants returned. He wanted to gather all the money that they had earned.
The man who received ten thousand dollars brought the other ten thousand dollars. He said, "Sir, you trusted me with ten thousand dollars. Look, I have earned an additional ten thousand dollars."
His master answered and said unto him, Well done, good and faithful servant; thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things; enter thou into the joy of thy lord!
The man to whom I had given four thousand dinars also came, and said, "My master, you gave me four thousand dinars, and here I have gained another four thousand."
His master answered and said unto him, Well done, good and faithful servant; thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord!
Then the slave who had been given the two thousand came and said, 'My master, I know that you are a harsh man, reaping where you did not sow and gathering where you did not scatter.'
I was scared, and I went out and put two thousand dollars on the ground. Look, this is what concerns you.
Then his master answered and said unto him, Thou wicked and lazy servant! Didst thou know that I gather where I have not sown, and I reap where I have not strawed?
Well, you should have put my money in the bank. When I came back, you would have received it with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants: "Take from him the two thousand dinars, and give them to the one who has twenty thousand dinars."
Everyone who has will be given more, and they will have abundance. But the one who has nothing will even lose what they have.
Cast out this good-for-nothing servant. Out there in the darkness, people will wail and gnash their teeth. 'Sheep and goats'
The Son of Man will come in His glory, and all the angels with Him. Then He will sit on the throne of His glory.
All the nations will be gathered before him, and he will separate the people one from another. As a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the King will say to those on His right, 'My sons, come to Me, that you may receive the Kingdom which the Lord your God has prepared for you from the beginning of the world.'
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
And the righteous answer him, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry and gave you food, or thirsty and gave you drink?'
When did we see you as a stranger and invite you into our homes? And when did we see you in need of clothes and give them to you?
When have we ever seen you sick or in prison and we went to visit you?
The King will reply, saying, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. Whatever you did for one of these little brothers of mine, you did for me."
Then he will say to those on his left: "Depart from me, ye cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels."
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger, and you did not welcome me. I needed clothes, and you did not give them to me. I was sick and in prison, and you did not take care of me.
They will also answer: 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not give you something to eat and drink? When did we see you as a stranger and did not take you in, or as someone without clothes and did not give you something to wear? When did we see you sick or in prison and did not visit you?'
He will reply by saying, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. Anything you did not do for one of the least of these, you did not do for me."
Then they go to be punished forever. As for those who did what is right, they will receive eternal life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus completed all these words, and then He said to His disciples:
You know, Easter is in two days. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
And they conspired to seize Jesus by guile and kill him.
But not during the feast," they said, "or the people might cause an uproar." A woman poured perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in Bethany at the home of Simon, who had been afflicted with leprosy.
A woman came to Jesus with an alabaster jar of very precious ointment, sealed. She poured the ointment on his head as he reclined.
When the disciples saw that, they disapproved, saying, "Why is this waste?"
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus knowing this said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has worked for me a beautiful work."
You will always have the poor with you. But you will not always have me.
I poured perfume on my body in preparation for my burial.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. What I have done will be told everywhere the good news of this salvation is preached to all the nations. It will be told for a memorial of her." Judas agrees to betray Jesus
And one of the twelve, whose name was Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests.
He said, "What will you give me if I were to hand him over to you?" They offered him thirty silver pieces.
From that time on, Judas watched for an opportune time to deliver Jesus over to them. The Lord's Supper
That was the first day of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover meal?"
He answered them, "Go to the city to So-and-so and say to him, 'The Teacher says, My time is near. I will keep the Passover with my disciples at your house.'"
So the disciples did as Jesus had instructed them. And they prepared the Passover.
And when evening came, Jesus was reclining at the table with the twelve disciples.
While they were eating, he said, "Truly I tell you, one of you will betray me to the enemies."
The disciples were deeply grieved. They began to say to him one after another, "Are you not I, Lord?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand with me in the bowl is the one who will betray me."
It is necessary for the Son of Man to go as it is written about him. Woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for that man if he had not been born.'
It was Judas who was going to betray him. He said, "Surely you are not one of them, are you, Teacher?" Jesus answered, "You have said so."
And they were eating, Jesus took bread, and having blessed, he broke it and gave it to the disciples, saying: "Take, eat. This is my body."
Then he took the cup and gave thanks and delivered it to them. And he said, "All of you drink from it."
This is my blood of the new covenant, shed for the forgiveness of sins for many.
This is what I tell you: from now on, I will not drink of the fruit of the vine until the day when I drink it with you new in the Kingdom of my Father.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "This very night before the rooster crows, you will all fall away because of me, for it is written: 'I will strike down the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered.' (Zechariah 13:7)"
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee."
Peter answered: "Everyone might leave because of you. But I will never do so."
Jesus answered, "What I am about to tell you is true. It will happen in this very night. Before the cock crows, you will deny knowing me three times."
But Peter said, "I may die with you, but I will never say that I don't know you." And all the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus' Prayer in Gethsemane
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. And he said to them, "Sit here, while I go there and pray."
And he took with him Peter and John and James, and he began to be sorrowful and troubled.
Then he said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful, even to death. Stay here and watch with me."
He went a little way forward, threw himself on the ground, and prayed, 'My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not as I will, but as you will.'
Then he returned to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Could you not watch with me for one hour?"
"Wait and be vigilant, and pray that you may be counted worthy to escape all these things that will come to pass, and to stand before the Son of Man. For I tell you that unless the Lord builds the house, they who build it labor in vain. Unless the Lord watches over the city, the watchman stays awake in vain. It is useless for a guard to go unarmed! If a stronger army attacks them, they will lose their reputation and be completely destroyed. But even if a church does not do these things and lives for the moment, it will not survive the coming persecution. Unless the Lord builds the city, its builders labor in vain."
Jesus went away a second time and prayed, saying, "My Father, if this cup cannot pass away unless I drink it, Your will be done."
Then he came back. He found them sleeping again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
And he left them and went away. And he prayed the third, which is the same prayer.
Then he returned to the disciples and said to them: "Are you still sleeping and resting? Behold, the hour is approaching, and the Son of Man will be betrayed into the hands of sinners."
"Get up! Let's go! Here he is, the one who is betraying me to them!" - The arrest of Jesus
While Jesus was still speaking, Judas, one of the twelve disciples, arrived with a large crowd armed with swords and clubs, sent by the chief priests and elders of the people.
Judas, who was about to betray Jesus, knew who they were. And he said, "The one I will kiss is the one; seize him."
Then Judas, one of the twelve, went to the chief priests in order to betray him to them. And when they heard it, they were glad, and promised to give him money. And he agreed, and sought an opportunity to betray him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do what you have come for." Then the men stepped forward. They seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword, struck the high priest's servant, and cut off his ear.
Jesus said, "Put your sword back in its place, for all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how do prophecies come true then? They say it must happen this way.
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowds, saying, "Am I a leader of a robber band, armed against you?" he asked. "Should you go out with swords and sticks to arrest me? I was sitting every day in the temple court teaching, and you did not arrest me."
But all this has happened so that the words of the prophets may be fulfilled." Then all the disciples left him and fled. Jesus is led to the council.
As for those who seized Jesus, they took him to Caiaphas, the chief priest. The scribes and elders had assembled there.
He was not very far away, so Peter followed Jesus. He went to the courtyard of the chief priest. He entered and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests and all the council of the Sanhedrin sought false witness against Jesus to put him to death.
But they found no evidence, although many false witnesses came forward. Finally, two other witnesses came forward.
They said: "This man said: 'I am able to destroy the temple of God. I can rebuild it once again in three days.'"
And the chief priest stood up and questioned Jesus, saying, "Do you not hear the testimony which these men are giving against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God to tell us: are you the Christ, the Son of God?"
Jesus answered, "Yes. Just as you say." "But now I say to all of you: In the coming days, you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the Power. You will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his clothes and said, "He has spoken blasphemy! Why do we still need witnesses? You have heard this blasphemous speech he made."
What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
Then they spat in his face. They struck him with their palms. And others slapped him.
They said, "Prophesy to us, Christ! Who is it that struck you?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard, and a servant girl came to him and said, "And you are one of Jesus of Nazareth's disciples."
But before them all, Peter said he was not. "I don't know what you are talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the path that led to the house, and a girl took him, another girl who was looking at him, and she said to the crowd: "This one was with Jesus of Nazareth."
He said again that he wasn't the one. He swore, saying, "I don't know this man!"
Soon, those who were standing there approached Peter and said to him, "Surely you are one of them, for how you speak gives you away."
Then Peter began to curse and swear, "I don't know this man you're talking about!" And immediately the cock crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said. "The cock will not crow," Jesus had told him. "Before the cock crows, you will deny me three times." Peter went out and wept bitterly.
Judas hangs himself.
It was early in the morning. All the chief priests and the elders of the people decided to kill Jesus.
And they bound him and led him away, and they delivered him to Pilate the governor.
Judas, who handed him over, and when he saw that Jesus was condemned to death, he repented and felt great shame for what he had done. He brought back the thirty-five pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders.
He said: "I made a mistake, I handed over an innocent man." They answered him: "What does it matter to us? This is your problem."
He threw the silver into the temple and departed. Then he went and bound himself.
The chief priests took the silver and said, "It is not lawful to put them into the treasury, for it is the price of blood."
So they discussed the matter and decided to buy a field for a burial plot, which was outside the city because he was also considered an outsider by the landowners.
Therefore, it was named "Garden of Blood" until this day.
And the word of the Lord came to Jeremiah, saying: 'Take from the hand of the scribe thirty silverlings, which are the price set by the children of Israel.'
They weighed out the money for the potter's field, as the Lord instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12, 13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) The Presentation of Jesus at the Temple They took the Child and His mother and presented Him at the temple, just as the custom was for every firstborn male. Their sacrifice, according to the law of the Lord, a pair of doves or two young pigeons, was brought to the Lord's priest at the entrance of the tent of meeting and sacrificed and burned for a pleasing aroma to the Lord.
Jesus was standing before the governor. And the governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the chief priests and elders accused him, he made no answer.
Then Pilate asked him, "Don't you hear the testimony they are giving against you?"
But Jesus answered nothing, not even to a single charge. And the governor was quite surprised.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose whom they wanted.
At that time they had a famous prisoner called Barabbas.
When the crowd was assembled, Pilate asked them, "Whom do you want me to release for you: Barabbas, or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were jealous. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting in the judgment hall, his wife sent him a message saying, "Have nothing to do with that righteous man, for I have suffered a great deal because of him today in a dream."
But the chief priests and the elders incited the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to ask for Jesus to be killed.
"Which do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
"What do you do with Jesus called the Christ?" Pilate asked. "Everyone answered: 'Crucify him!'"
"Why? What crime has he committed?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that it was of no avail; indeed, the crowd was getting angry. He took water and washed his hands in their sight, saying, "I am innocent of the blood of this man; see to it yourselves!"
All the people answered: "We and our children will bear responsibility for his death!"
Pilate released Barabbas. But he flogged Jesus and then handed him over to be crucified. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers seized Jesus and brought him into the governor's residence, known as the palace. All the soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him and put a purple robe on him.
Then they intertwined the thorns together to make a crown. And they put it on his head. And they put a reed in his right hand. Then they knelt down before him, each one mocking him, saying, "Hail, King of the Jews!"
They spat on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After mocking him, they stripped him of his robe. They put back on him his own clothes. Then they led him to be crucified on the cross. Jesus is crucified on the cross.
And as they were bringing him out of the city, they found a man from Cyrene named Simon, and they compelled him to carry his cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
And they mixed wine with myrrh and offered it to him to drink. And when he tasted it, he would not agree to drink it.
And when they crucified him, they divided his garments among themselves and cast lots for them.
They sat and kept watch over him there.
And they placed over his head a title written: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
And two robbers were crucified with Him, one on His right and the other on His left.
The bystanders yelled at Jesus and mocked him. They shook their heads
And they said, "Are you to save yourself? If you are the Son of God, come down from the cross!"
Likewise, the chief priests, the scribes, and the elders mocked him.
"It is true that he has saved others," they said, "but he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross, and we will believe in him."
Let him who believes in God save him now, if he will; he said, "I am the Son of God."
In the same way, the two thieves who were being crucified with Jesus also mocked him. Jesus dies.
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" which means "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who stood there heard how Jesus cried out, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" And some of the bystanders, hearing it, said, "This is Elijah."
One of them ran quickly and brought a soaked sponge with vinegar, placed it on a reed, and offered it to Jesus to drink.
And the rest said, "Leave him alone. Let us see whether Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
And at that moment, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. And the earth shook, and the rocks were split.
And the tombs were opened, and many of the bodies of the saints who had been sleeping arose.
They came out of the tombs after the resurrection of Jesus, and they entered the holy city and appeared to many.
The commander of the temple guard and the men who were guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that happened; and they were filled with fear and said, "Truly, he was the Son of God!"
Not long ago, there were many women watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to serve him and meet his needs.
Mary Magdalene was there. There was also Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus was buried.
And when evening came, a rich man came from Arimathea, whose name was Joseph, and he was also a disciple of Jesus.
So he went to Pilate and requested the body of Jesus. Pilate commanded that the body be given to him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean shroud.
And he placed it in a new tomb which he had excavated from the rock, and he rolled a large stone over the mouth of the tomb. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there in front of the tomb. The guards of the tomb
The next day, after the day of preparation, the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
They said, "Sir, we remember something that liar said when he was alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
Therefore, order that the tomb be made secure until the third day, lest his disciples come at night and steal him away, and say to the people, "He is risen from the dead," then the last deception would be worse than the first.
Pilate answered, "Have some guards with you. Go, make the tomb as secure as you can."
So they went and made the grave secure, sealing the stone and placing guards over it.
Jesus rises from the dead.
It was the end of the Sabbath, and the beginning of the first day of the week. And Mary the Magdalene and the other Mary came to see the tomb.
A violent earthquake occurred. The Angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He rolled away the stone and sat on it.
His body shone like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards feared him so much that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to them, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are seeking Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen, as He said! Come, see the place where He was lying.
Go at once and tell his disciples: 'He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him.' I have told you.'
The women quickly departed from the tomb, trembling and utterly terrified, and they ran to inform his disciples.
Suddenly, Jesus met them. "Peace be with you!" he said. So they came to him, and they took hold of his feet and worshiped him.
Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The guards went and informed the chief priests.
And as the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city and reported to the chief priests all that had happened.
When the chief priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
Tell the soldiers, "We want you to say, 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we will push it. This will keep you out of trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story spread among the Jews until this day. The final instructions of Jesus to his disciples.
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had told them to go.
When they saw him, they bowed down to him. But some still had their doubts.
Then Jesus came and spoke to them, saying, "All authority has been given to Me in heaven and on earth."
Go ye therefore and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Ghost.
Teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. And know that I am with you always, to the end."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote, saying, "I send My messenger before Your face, Who will prepare Your way before You." (Malachi 3:1)
A voice cries out in the wilderness: "Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight." (Isaiah 40:3)
Then John came and baptized in the wilderness. And he was preaching, saying: "If you want to receive the thought of God, then be baptized and God will forgive you your sins."
All the people from every part of Judea and all the people of Jerusalem went out to him. They confessed their sins, and John baptized them in the river Jordan, as they confessed their sins.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He wore a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to preach: "He who comes after me is mightier than I. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of His sandals."
"I baptize you with water; but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The Baptism and Temptation of Jesus
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and John baptized him in the Jordan River.
And Jesus, when he was baptized, went up immediately from the water, and behold, the heavens were opened to him, and he saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and coming upon him.
A voice spoke to him from heaven, saying, "You are my Son, whom I love; with pleasure I approve of you."
Immediately the Spirit drove Jesus into the wilderness.
He had been in the desert for forty days. There, he was tempted by the devil. Wild animals did not harm him, but angels looked after him. Jesus chose his first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went to Galilee, where he preached the good news of God.
"The time has come," he said. "The Kingdom of God is at hand. Repent of your sins and believe in the good news!"
One day, Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
Then Jesus walked a little way ahead of them, and saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John, who were in a boat mending their nets.
And He called them immediately. So they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired hands, and went after Jesus.
Jesus and those with him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue and began to teach.
And the people were astonished at his teaching, for he was teaching them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
At that moment, a man in their synagogue, who was possessed by an evil spirit, cried out,
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!"
"Be quiet!" said Jesus firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a scream.
And all the people were amazed and were questioning among themselves, saying, "What is this? For a new teaching is with authority! And he commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey him!"
The news about Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the synagogue, and immediately, together with James and John, they went to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed, suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand and helped her rise. The fever left her, then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all the sick. They also brought all those who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them, as they had various diseases. He also cast out many demons, and He did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who He was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went looking for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the nearby villages. I must preach there also. That is why I have come."
And Jesus went throughout all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing all manner of sickness and all manner of disease and weakness among the people. And his fame went throughout all Syria; and they brought unto him all sick people that were taken with divers diseases and pains, and possessed with devils, and those that were lunatick, and paralytic; and he healed them. And there followed him great multitudes of people from Galilee, and from Decapolis, and from Jerusalem, and from Judea, and from beyond the Jordan.
A man who had a skin disease came to Jesus. He fell on his knees and pleaded with Jesus, saying, "If you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be 'clean'!"
Immediately, the illness left him. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent him away. The man was given a strong warning.
"Don't tell anyone about this," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. That will be a testimony to the priest and the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to speak about him openly, and spread the news about him to everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter a city openly. And he remained outside in isolated places. But the people were coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. And the people heard that he was at home.
Many people gathered until there was no more room. And there wasn't even any room outside the door. And Jesus preached the word to them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not get him near Jesus because of the crowd. So they went up on the roof and, since it was flat, they made an opening in it. Then they let down the mat on which the paralytic was lying.
Jesus saw their faith. And he said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
And he sat there, a scribe from the Pharisees, thinking about him:
Why does this man talk like this? He is saying something very wicked! Only God can forgive sins!
Immediately Jesus knew what they were thinking in their hearts. And he said to them, "Why are you thinking about these things?
Should I say to this one, 'Your sins are forgiven'? Or should I say, 'Rise and walk'?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then He said to the paralytic:
He said, "I'm telling you, get up. Take your mat and go home."
He rose, took his mat, and went out. And everyone was astonished, and they glorified God saying, "We never saw anything like this!" The Choice of Jesus the Levi
And Jesus returned again to the sea of Galilee. And a great multitude gathered unto him, so he entered into a ship and sat down, and all the people stood on the shore.
And as he walked, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax collection site. And he said to him, Follow me. And he got up and followed him.
And afterward, Jesus was dining at the house of Levi. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
There were some Pharisee teachers of the law. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. They asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this, and said to them, "The healthy do not need a physician, but the sick. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners, to repentance and following me." (Jesus is asked about fasting)
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. And the Pharisees came to Him and said, "The disciples of John fast often and make prayers. And the disciples of the Pharisees also do the same. But Your disciples fast not. Why is this?"
Jesus answered and said to them, "Can the groomsmen fast while the bridegroom is with them? As long as the bridegroom is with them, they cannot fast.
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will fast.
People do not sew a patch of new cloth onto old clothes. If they did, the new piece would tear away from the old. That would make the tear worse.
People don't put new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the wineskins. Then the wine is ruined and the wineskins are destroyed. No, people put new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath day.
One Sabbath, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the wheat fields. And the disciples began to pluck some of the wheat spikes.
The Pharisees said to him, "Look! It is not permitted to do this on the Sabbath, according to the law. Why then do your disciples do this?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the holy showbread. Only the priests were permitted to eat it. David also gave some to his men.
Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man's sake, and not man for the Sabbath's sake."
So the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath.
Healing on the Sabbath.
On another day, Jesus entered the synagogue. There was a man there whose right hand was withered and deformed.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They watched him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and shriveled hand. "Come forward," he said.
Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say in such a case? For it is lawful to do good on the Sabbath, to save life rather than to kill?" But they remained silent.
Jesus looked at them with a look of anger, for he was very grieved by the hardness of their hearts. He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was restored sound and well as the other.
Then the Pharisees went out and held a council with the Herodians against Jesus, to catch him in his words. And they were seeking to kill him. And many crowds were following Jesus.
Jesus, setting out with his disciples for the Sea of Galilee, was followed by a great crowd of people from Galilee.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him from Judea and Jerusalem and from all around the region of the Jordan Valley. Large crowds also came from the region of Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus told his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from crowding him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were crowding to touch him.
And as for those who do not believe, it is words that are similar to each other that will put their noses to the grindstone. And they do not know what they are saying. And when the unclean spirit sees him, he falls down before him and shouts: "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up into the mountain, and called his disciples, and they came unto him.
He appointed twelve of them, and called them apostles. And they were with him from that time on. And he also sent them out to preach.
They will have authority to cast out demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve, and he gave Simon the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name "Boanerges." Boanerges means "sons of thunder."
And Judas Thaddeus, and Simon the Canaanite, and Judas Iscariot, who also betrayed him. And Thaddeus, and Simon the Zealot.
Jesus and Beelzebul
Jesus entered a house. The crowd gathered again, and they were so numerous that Jesus and His disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about it. So they went to take control. They said, "He has lost his mind."
And there came scribes from Jerusalem, saying, "He is possessed by Beelzebub, and by the prince of the devils casteth he out devils."
Jesus called them all and said to them, "If Satan casts out Satan, he is divided; his kingdom cannot stand."
If a kingdom fights against itself, it will not endure.
If a family splits, it cannot endure.
